<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spanish–American War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Spanish-American War</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spanish–American War was fought in 1898 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, arising from the Cuban struggle for independence. Spain was quickly defeated and lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, Guam and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> treat it as the end of Spain's overseas empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war.jpg" alt="Spanish–American War" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Barbudo Barbudo and TEP · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Collage_infobox_for_Spanish-American_War.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Spanish–American War</b>, Spain ceded Cuba, Puerto Rico, Guam and the Philippines to the United States by the Treaty of Paris of 1898 for US$20 million, and Cuba, which had declared independence from the Spanish Empire that year, passed under an American military government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-cuba-1902-1959">Republic of Cuba (1902-1959) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Spanish-American War</b> of 1898, in which the United States destroyed Spanish squadrons at Manila Bay and Santiago de Cuba within months, stripped Spain of these last major overseas possessions, completing an imperial dissolution that had begun nearly a century earlier, although small African territories remained.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1898, during the <b>Spanish-American War</b>, the cruiser USS Charleston captured the island, and Spain ceded it to the United States.</blockquote><a href="/state/guam">Guam</a></li><li><blockquote>American troops landed at Guánica in July 1898 during the <b>Spanish-American War</b>, months after Spain had granted Puerto Rico an autonomous charter.</blockquote><a href="/state/puerto-rico-united-states">Puerto Rico (United States)</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Spanish-American War</b> made Hawaii strategically valuable, and after a treaty failed in the Senate despite petitions by many Native Hawaiians, Congress annexed the islands by the Newlands Resolution in July 1898.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-hawaii">Republic of Hawaii</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Grito de Lares revolt of 1868, Spain granted autonomy in 1897, but the United States invaded in 1898 during the <b>Spanish-American War</b>, and the Treaty of Paris transferred the island to Puerto Rico (United States).</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-puerto-rico">Spanish Puerto Rico</a></li></ul></section>
